{"kind":"artifact","text":"The committed embed is rebuilt from the repaired browser source, so the resident serves the authority repair rather than the build that predates it. Exact clean pushed head f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c on request/decision-records-ui-recut; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 340806ed, checkout clean. 5 files, +14/-14: the hashed JS and CSS bundles replaced and index.html re-pointed at them.\n\nWHY THIS PATH HAS ITS OWN ARTIFACT. The resident serves this committed directory, not ui/src. An edit to the browser source that is not accompanied by a rebuild here leaves the running workroom on the old bundle, so the authority repair recorded in artifact bbdf6c6b would be true of the source and false of what an operator actually sees. That failure is silent: every test over ui/src passes while the served screen still offers the refused action.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED, at this exact head, rather than trusting the build. I ran make ui and then git status: it reported zero entries. The embed is therefore byte-reproduced from the committed source, and the bundles here are the ones this source produces. make ui-check exited 0. The head SHA is unchanged by that rebuild, which is the point — had make ui altered anything, the tree would have moved and this artifact would be naming a commit that no longer describes the build.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD. make ui exit 0 with an empty git status; make ui-check exit 0; npm test --prefix ui 97 tests, 97 pass, 0 fail; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 33 packages, zero failures, zero cached; gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0 with no output; git diff --check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c","path":"internal/service/uidist"}}